<EXPERIMENT_PACKAGE><EXPERIMENT accession="SRX17732405" alias="GSM6604338_r1"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRX17732405</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="GEO">GSM6604338_r1</EXTERNAL_ID></IDENTIFIERS><TITLE>GSM6604338: CTL_NAWM_03; Homo sapiens; RNA-Seq</TITLE><STUDY_REF accession="SRP399985"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRP399985</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="BioProject">PRJNA885014</EXTERNAL_ID></IDENTIFIERS></STUDY_REF><DESIGN><DESIGN_DESCRIPTION/><SAMPLE_DESCRIPTOR accession="SRS15261389"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRS15261389</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="GEO">GSM6604338</EXTERNAL_ID></IDENTIFIERS></SAMPLE_DESCRIPTOR><LIBRARY_DESCRIPTOR><LIBRARY_NAME>GSM6604338</LIBRARY_NAME><LIBRARY_STRATEGY>RNA-Seq</LIBRARY_STRATEGY><LIBRARY_SOURCE>TRANSCRIPTOMIC</LIBRARY_SOURCE><LIBRARY_SELECTION>cDNA</LIBRARY_SELECTION><LIBRARY_LAYOUT><SINGLE/></LIBRARY_LAYOUT><LIBRARY_CONSTRUCTION_PROTOCOL>Normal-appearing white matter was dissected from the obtained tissue block and total RNA was subsequently harvested using the TriReagent RNA extraction protocol. 1ug of total RNA per sample was subsequently used for the contructio of the sequencing library. Library preparation was performed at the Ramaciotti Centre for Genomics (Sydney,NSW) using Illumina® Stranded Total RNA with Ribo-Zero Plus.</LIBRARY_CONSTRUCTION_PROTOCOL></LIBRARY_DESCRIPTOR></DESIGN><PLATFORM><ILLUMINA><INSTRUMENT_MODEL>Illumina NovaSeq 6000</INSTRUMENT_MODEL></ILLUMINA></PLATFORM></EXPERIMENT><SUBMISSION lab_name="" center_name="University of Technology Sydney" accession="SRA1508704" alias="SUB12107131"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRA1508704</PRIMARY_ID><SUBMITTER_ID namespace="University of Technology Sydney">SUB12107131</SUBMITTER_ID></IDENTIFIERS></SUBMISSION><Organization type="institute"><Name>University of Technology Sydney</Name><Contact email="alenfaiz1@gmail.com"><Address><Street>Thomas St,</Street><City>Ultimo</City><Sub>NSW x</Sub><Country>Australia</Country></Address><Name><First>Alen</First><Last>Faiz</Last></Name></Contact></Organization><STUDY center_name="GEO" alias="GSE214334" accession="SRP399985"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRP399985</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="BioProject" label="primary">PRJNA885014</EXTERNAL_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="GEO">GSE214334</EXTERNAL_ID></IDENTIFIERS><DESCRIPTOR><STUDY_TITLE>Transcriptomic analyses of normal-appearing CNS white matter from multiple sclerosis donors reveal subtype-specific molecular signatures of disease</STUDY_TITLE><STUDY_TYPE existing_study_type="Transcriptome Analysis"/><STUDY_ABSTRACT>The transcriptome of normal-appearing white matter for relapse-remitting multiple sclerosis (MS), primary progressive MS and secondary progressive MS was determined using total RNA-sequencing. We then performed a differential gene analysis comparing the normal-appearing white matter for each clinical subtype of MS with non-MS control tissue Overall design: Comparative analysis of the transcriptional profiles of normal-appearing white matter obtained from non-MS and MS donors diagnosed with different clinical subtypes of disease (RRMS, SPMS, PPMS)</STUDY_ABSTRACT><CENTER_PROJECT_NAME>GSE214334</CENTER_PROJECT_NAME></DESCRIPTOR></STUDY><SAMPLE alias="GSM6604338" accession="SRS15261389"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRS15261389</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="BioSample">SAMN31062711</EXTERNAL_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="GEO">GSM6604338</EXTERNAL_ID></IDENTIFIERS><TITLE>CTL_NAWM_03</TITLE><SAMPLE_NAME><TAXON_ID>9606</TAXON_ID><SCIENTIFIC_NAME>Homo sapiens</SCIENTIFIC_NAME></SAMPLE_NAME><SAMPLE_LINKS><SAMPLE_LINK><XREF_LINK><DB>bioproject</DB><ID>885014</ID><LABEL>PRJNA885014</LABEL></XREF_LINK></SAMPLE_LINK></SAMPLE_LINKS><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTES><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>source_name</TAG><VALUE>Brain white matter</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>tissue</TAG><VALUE>Brain white matter</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>disease state</TAG><VALUE>Control</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTES></SAMPLE><P